event: response.function_call_arguments.done
data: {"type":"response.function_call_arguments.done","sequence_number":1176,"item_id":"fc_0c17af8fc60a9be3016adb5154cef88191a31c129812a1161c","output_index":1,"arguments":"{\"artifact\":{\"base_input\":\"prior-candidate.json\",\"reset_base\":true,\"patches\":[{\"op\":\"replace\",\"path\":\"/graph/propositions/9/source/declaration\",\"value\":\"Real.volume_Icc\"},{\"op\":\"replace\",\"path\":\"/graph/propositions/9/source/module\",\"value\":\"Mathlib.MeasureTheory.Measure.Lebesgue.Basic\"},{\"op\":\"add\",\"path\":\"/graph/propositions/-\",\"value\":{\"id\":\"M013\",\"kind\":\"mathlib\",\"statement\":\"For an outer measure μ and sets A⊆B, μ(A)≤μ(B).\",\"definitions\":[],\"binders\":[],\"objects\":[],\"source\":{\"statement\":\"For an outer measure μ and sets A⊆B, μ(A)≤μ(B).\",\"hypotheses\":[\"μ is an outer measure; A and B are sets with A⊆B.\"],\"declaration\":\"MeasureTheory.measure_mono\",\"module\":\"Mathlib.MeasureTheory.OuterMeasure.Basic\"}}},{\"op\":\"add\",\"path\":\"/graph/inferences/0/inputs/-\",\"value\":\"M013\"},{\"op\":\"add\",\"path\":\"/graph/inferences/0/applications/-\",\"value\":{\"input\":\"M013\",\"at\":\"S002\",\"bindings\":\"μ is Lebesgue measure; A=J_i∩[0,1], B is a closed interval containing this set.\",\"hypothesis_checks\":[\"A⊆B by the interval enclosure; Lebesgue measure is an outer measure.\"]}},{\"op\":\"add\",\"path\":\"/graph/inferences/0/argument/1/uses/-\",\"value\":\"M013\"},{\"op\":\"replace\",\"path\":\"/graph/inferences/0/argument/1/text\",\"value\":\"For D>0 fix 0<s<D. For a>0 define C_a(A,δ) to be the infimum of ∑'ᵢ ediam(Uᵢ)^a over all Nat-indexed sequences with A⊆⋃ᵢUᵢ and ediam(Uᵢ)≤δ. Empty members have diameter zero and cost zero. In M008 the nonempty-witness supremum is ediam(Uᵢ)^a for a nonempty member and zero for an empty member, so its nested infimum is exactly C_a(A,δ); hence H^a(A)=sup_{δ>0}C_a(A,δ), with no factor. If δ₁≤δ₂, admissible covers at δ₁ are a subset of those at δ₂, so C_a(A,δ₁)≥C_a(A,δ₂). Thus the decreasing-scale limit equals the supremum. M001 gives H^s(E)=∞, so C_s(E,δ) tends to infinity as δ↓0. Fix any countable δ-cover Vᵢ of F and put ρᵢ=ediam(Vᵢ), Aᵢ=π₁(Vᵢ), Bᵢ=π₂(Vᵢ). Projections do not increase diameter. Choose ηᵢ=ε·2^(−i−1)/(1+ρᵢ^s)>0 and an open interval Jᵢ containing Bᵢ with length ≤ρᵢ+ηᵢ; boundedness follows from diam(Bᵢ)≤ρᵢ, and the interval is obtained from its infimum/supremum with total added width ηᵢ. For each t∈[0,1] and x∈E, (x,t) lies in some Vᵢ, so x∈Aᵢ and t∈Bᵢ⊆Jᵢ. Hence the Aᵢ indexed by t∈Jᵢ cover E and C_s(E,δ)≤∑'ᵢ1_{Jᵢ∩[0,1]}(t)ρᵢ^s pointwise. Use ν=volume.restrict([0,1]); its ambient measure space is ℝ with Borel measurable sets, and ν is finite since ν([0,1])=volume([0,1])=1 by M011 and the definition of restriction. Each Jᵢ∩[0,1] is Borel, so the indicator times ρᵢ^s is measurable and therefore AEMeasurable for ν. Integrate the pointwise inequality using M009 and apply M002 to interchange the countable tsum and lintegral. M010 evaluates each summand as ρᵢ^sν(Jᵢ∩[0,1]). This restricted measure is at most the Lebesgue measure of the same set, and the set is contained in an enclosing closed interval [aᵢ,bᵢ] of length ≤ρᵢ+ηᵢ. Apply M013 to compare its Lebesgue measure with the enclosing interval measure, then M011 gives that measure as ofReal(bᵢ−aᵢ)≤ofReal(ρᵢ+ηᵢ). Consequently C_s(E,δ)≤∑'ᵢρᵢ^(s+1)+∑'ᵢηᵢρᵢ^s≤∑'ᵢρᵢ^(s+1)+ε, since ∑ᵢ2^(−i−1)≤1. This holds for every ε>0; if the cover's (s+1)-cost is finite, taking ε↓0 gives C_s(E,δ)≤that cost, and if infinite it is immediate. Taking the infimum over all such covers yields C_s(E,δ)≤C_(s+1)(F,δ). As δ↓0 the left tends to infinity, so the right does also; M008 then gives H^(s+1)(F)=∞. Apply M001 with NNReal exponent s+1 to get s+1≤dim_H(F). Since this holds for every 0<s<D, density of the reals gives D+1≤dim_H(F). Coercions use the NNReal exponent in dimension theorems and its corresponding real coercion in M008.\"}]}}"}

event: response.output_item.done
data: {"type":"response.output_item.done","sequence_number":1177,"output_index":1,"item":{"type":"function_call","id":"fc_0c17af8fc60a9be3016adb5154cef88191a31c129812a1161c","call_id":"call_be37eee8edbb41eba981cb6bd9b17ea3","name":"check_draft","arguments":"{\"artifact\":{\"base_input\":\"prior-candidate.json\",\"reset_base\":true,\"patches\":[{\"op\":\"replace\",\"path\":\"/graph/propositions/9/source/declaration\",\"value\":\"Real.volume_Icc\"},{\"op\":\"replace\",\"path\":\"/graph/propositions/9/source/module\",\"value\":\"Mathlib.MeasureTheory.Measure.Lebesgue.Basic\"},{\"op\":\"add\",\"path\":\"/graph/propositions/-\",\"value\":{\"id\":\"M013\",\"kind\":\"mathlib\",\"statement\":\"For an outer measure μ and sets A⊆B, μ(A)≤μ(B).\",\"definitions\":[],\"binders\":[],\"objects\":[],\"source\":{\"statement\":\"For an outer measure μ and sets A⊆B, μ(A)≤μ(B).\",\"hypotheses\":[\"μ is an outer measure; A and B are sets with A⊆B.\"],\"declaration\":\"MeasureTheory.measure_mono\",\"module\":\"Mathlib.MeasureTheory.OuterMeasure.Basic\"}}},{\"op\":\"add\",\"path\":\"/graph/inferences/0/inputs/-\",\"value\":\"M013\"},{\"op\":\"add\",\"path\":\"/graph/inferences/0/applications/-\",\"value\":{\"input\":\"M013\",\"at\":\"S002\",\"bindings\":\"μ is Lebesgue measure; A=J_i∩[0,1], B is a closed interval containing this set.\",\"hypothesis_checks\":[\"A⊆B by the interval enclosure; Lebesgue measure is an outer measure.\"]}},{\"op\":\"add\",\"path\":\"/graph/inferences/0/argument/1/uses/-\",\"value\":\"M013\"},{\"op\":\"replace\",\"path\":\"/graph/inferences/0/argument/1/text\",\"value\":\"For D>0 fix 0<s<D. For a>0 define C_a(A,δ) to be the infimum of ∑'ᵢ ediam(Uᵢ)^a over all Nat-indexed sequences with A⊆⋃ᵢUᵢ and ediam(Uᵢ)≤δ. Empty members have diameter zero and cost zero. In M008 the nonempty-witness supremum is ediam(Uᵢ)^a for a nonempty member and zero for an empty member, so its nested infimum is exactly C_a(A,δ); hence H^a(A)=sup_{δ>0}C_a(A,δ), with no factor. If δ₁≤δ₂, admissible covers at δ₁ are a subset of those at δ₂, so C_a(A,δ₁)≥C_a(A,δ₂). Thus the decreasing-scale limit equals the supremum. M001 gives H^s(E)=∞, so C_s(E,δ) tends to infinity as δ↓0. Fix any countable δ-cover Vᵢ of F and put ρᵢ=ediam(Vᵢ), Aᵢ=π₁(Vᵢ), Bᵢ=π₂(Vᵢ). Projections do not increase diameter. Choose ηᵢ=ε·2^(−i−1)/(1+ρᵢ^s)>0 and an open interval Jᵢ containing Bᵢ with length ≤ρᵢ+ηᵢ; boundedness follows from diam(Bᵢ)≤ρᵢ, and the interval is obtained from its infimum/supremum with total added width ηᵢ. For each t∈[0,1] and x∈E, (x,t) lies in some Vᵢ, so x∈Aᵢ and t∈Bᵢ⊆Jᵢ. Hence the Aᵢ indexed by t∈Jᵢ cover E and C_s(E,δ)≤∑'ᵢ1_{Jᵢ∩[0,1]}(t)ρᵢ^s pointwise. Use ν=volume.restrict([0,1]); its ambient measure space is ℝ with Borel measurable sets, and ν is finite since ν([0,1])=volume([0,1])=1 by M011 and the definition of restriction. Each Jᵢ∩[0,1] is Borel, so the indicator times ρᵢ^s is measurable and therefore AEMeasurable for ν. Integrate the pointwise inequality using M009 and apply M002 to interchange the countable tsum and lintegral. M010 evaluates each summand as ρᵢ^sν(Jᵢ∩[0,1]). This restricted measure is at most the Lebesgue measure of the same set, and the set is contained in an enclosing closed interval [aᵢ,bᵢ] of length ≤ρᵢ+ηᵢ. Apply M013 to compare its Lebesgue measure with the enclosing interval measure, then M011 gives that measure as ofReal(bᵢ−aᵢ)≤ofReal(ρᵢ+ηᵢ). Consequently C_s(E,δ)≤∑'ᵢρᵢ^(s+1)+∑'ᵢηᵢρᵢ^s≤∑'ᵢρᵢ^(s+1)+ε, since ∑ᵢ2^(−i−1)≤1. This holds for every ε>0; if the cover's (s+1)-cost is finite, taking ε↓0 gives C_s(E,δ)≤that cost, and if infinite it is immediate. Taking the infimum over all such covers yields C_s(E,δ)≤C_(s+1)(F,δ). As δ↓0 the left tends to infinity, so the right does also; M008 then gives H^(s+1)(F)=∞. Apply M001 with NNReal exponent s+1 to get s+1≤dim_H(F). Since this holds for every 0<s<D, density of the reals gives D+1≤dim_H(F). Coercions use the NNReal exponent in dimension theorems and its corresponding real coercion in M008.\"}]}}","status":"completed"}}

